    "updated_at": "2018-09-27T21:50:43Z",
    "body": "This was certainly a problem in Chrome and Safari for a time, but I believe it's no longer the case in either browser (I'd have to test to confirm). Whether a lack of clarity in the spec was the cause, I couldn't say, but I do think the spec describes the intended/desired behaviour.\r\n\r\nThis seems to be the critical step:\r\n>Move the [sequential focus navigation starting point](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interaction.html#sequential-focus-navigation-starting-point) to target.>\r\n\r\nThe description for [sequential focus navigation](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interaction.html#sequential-focus-navigation) notes that:\r\n>Typically, pressing tab requests the next control, and pressing shift+tab requests the previous control.>\r\n\r\nSo when the sequential focus navigation starting point is set to the element with the fragment identifier, the next press of the tab key should take keyboard focus to the next focusable element in the focus navigation order.\r\n\r\n\r\nUnless browsers treat sequential focus navigation and keyboard focus as separate things, I think the step makes the required action clear. It might be worth making a small clarification change to the spec text though, just for \"belt and braces\":\r\n>Set the [sequential focus navigation starting point](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interaction.html#sequential-focus-navigation-starting-point) to the target.>\r\n\r\nThe words \"set\" and \"move\" are interchangeable in this context, but \"set\" might perhaps make the intended action more apparent.\r\n\r\nI also note that the step numbering appears to have changed since @takenspc filed this issue, so perhaps someone has already resolved this (intentionally or otherwise)?",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
